---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { feeds, type Category } from '../feeds.config';

const categories = [...new Set(feeds.map((f) => f.category))] as Category[];
---

<Base title="About · hi jon">
  <main class="container prose">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      hi jon is a small, quiet reader for the writing I actually want to keep up with: frontend,
      tooling and AI, gathered into one feed.
    </p>

    <p>
      Good writing about the web is spread across newsletters, blogs and changelogs. Some land in an
      inbox that is already too full. Some only exist as a link on social media, and some are easy to
      forget entirely. I wanted one place to look each morning that didn't come with an algorithm,
      notifications or a reason to keep scrolling once I'd caught up.
    </p>

    <p>
      So this is deliberately simple. Every source is fetched directly from its RSS or Atom feed,
      merged into a single list and shown newest first. Nothing is ranked, recommended or hidden. Items
      you've opened fade back so new ones stand out, and that state lives only in your browser. There
      are no accounts and no tracking.
    </p>

    <h2>What's in it</h2>
    {categories.map((category) => (
      <p>
        <strong>{category}:</strong>{' '}
        {feeds
          .filter((f) => f.category === category)
          .map((f) => f.name)
          .join(', ')}
      </p>
    ))}

    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
      The page is rendered on request and cached at the edge for about fifteen minutes, then quietly
      refreshed in the background. If a feed is slow or down, it's skipped rather than holding up the
      rest.
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>
